<div class="wrap prose">

  <section id="short" aria-labelledby="short-h">
    <h2 id="short-h">The short version</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>Study</strong> for the entry-level <em>Technician</em> exam. Plenty of free material exists.</li>
      <li><strong>Get an FRN</strong>: a free FCC Registration Number, which you need before the exam.</li>
      <li><strong>Take the exam</strong> in person or online with a team of volunteer examiners.</li>
      <li><strong>Pay the FCC's application fee</strong> after you pass, and receive your callsign.</li>
      <li><strong>Get on the air</strong> and say hello. <a href="/events/">Come to a net or a meeting</a>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>You don't need a license to listen, and you don't need to own a radio to start studying.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="classes" aria-labelledby="classes-h">
    <h2 id="classes-h">The three license classes</h2>
    <p>
      There are three levels. Most people start with Technician and upgrade later if they want more.
      Each exam is a written, multiple-choice test, and you pass with 74 percent or better.
    </p>
    <ul class="cards">
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Technician</h3>
        <p class="muted">Entry level · 35 questions</p>
        <p>
          Local and regional communication on the VHF and UHF bands, which is where handheld radios and
          repeaters live, plus limited access to a few shortwave (HF) bands. This is where nearly everyone begins.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>General</h3>
        <p class="muted">Step up · 35 questions</p>
        <p>
          Much broader shortwave (HF) privileges, which is the part of the hobby that reaches other
          states and countries.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li class="card">
        <h3>Amateur Extra</h3>
        <p class="muted">Top class · 50 questions</p>
        <p>
          All privileges on all amateur bands. It has the hardest exam, and it's a big
          accomplishment even for longtime hams.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      There is no Morse code requirement for any class. Licenses last ten years and can be renewed.
      You can take more than one exam in a single session if you pass the first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="exams" aria-labelledby="exams-h">
    <h2 id="exams-h">How exams work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Who runs them.</strong> Exams are given by teams of <strong>Volunteer Examiners (VEs)</strong>:
        licensed hams accredited through a <strong>Volunteer Examiner Coordinator (VEC)</strong> such as the
        <a href="https://www.arrl.org/getting-licensed" rel="noopener">ARRL</a>. The people grading your
        test are volunteers, usually friendly and encouraging.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The questions are public.</strong> Every exam is drawn from a published pool of questions
        and answers, so studying the pool is fair game. Pools are refreshed every few years.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Get an FRN first.</strong> New applicants must have an FCC Registration Number before the
        exam. You register for free through the FCC's CORES system; ARRL
        <a href="https://arrl.org/news/first-time-exam-applicants-must-obtain-fcc-registration-number-before-taking-exam" rel="noopener">explains the requirement</a>.
        If you already hold an FCC license of any kind, you already have one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>What to bring.</strong> Typically a photo ID, your FRN, the exam fee, a pen or pencil, and a
        simple calculator. Check the session listing for anything specific. Upgrading hams also bring their
        existing license or exam credit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Two fees.</strong> The VE team charges a small session fee. ARRL VEC sessions have charged
        <strong>$15</strong>; check each listing. After you pass, the FCC charges its own application fee
        (<strong>$35</strong> when it began in April 2022). The FCC emails you a payment link, and
        <a href="https://www.arrl.org/news/new-amateur-radio-license-applications-fee-to-become-effective-april-19-2022" rel="noopener">ARRL's announcement</a>
        has the details. Fee amounts can change, so confirm them before you go.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>After you pass.</strong> The FCC processes your application and assigns a callsign. You can
        generally begin transmitting once your callsign appears in the FCC's license database (called
        <strong>ULS</strong>).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="find" aria-labelledby="find-h">
    <h2 id="find-h">Find an exam session near Middletown</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://www.arrl.org/find-an-amateur-radio-license-exam-session" rel="noopener">ARRL's exam finder</a>
        lets you search by ZIP code within 25, 50, 100, or 250 miles, or by state. It also lists online exams you can take from home.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://hamstudy.org/" rel="noopener">HamStudy.org</a> also lists exam sessions, alongside its study tools.
      </li>
      <li>Exam sessions I know about locally appear on the <a href="/events/">Events page</a> (look for the <span class="tag">Exam session</span> tag).</li>
    </ul>
