  <section id="study" aria-labelledby="study-h">
    <h2 id="study-h">Study resources</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Practice exams (free).</strong> <a href="https://hamstudy.org/" rel="noopener">HamStudy.org</a> and
        <a href="https://hamexam.org/" rel="noopener">HamExam.org</a> let you take practice tests drawn from the real question pools.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Books.</strong> ARRL publishes <em>The ARRL Ham Radio License Manual</em> and a
        <em>Tech Q&amp;A</em> guide, and Gordon West's <em>Technician Class License Prep</em> is another popular choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Classes.</strong> ARRL has a <a href="https://www.arrl.org/getting-licensed" rel="noopener">“Find a Class” search</a>,
        and local clubs often run beginner courses.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Match your materials to the current question pool.</strong> The 2026–2030 Technician pool took
      effect on July 1, 2026, and study guides based on the older pool no longer match the exam
      (<a href="https://www.arrl.org/news/new-technician-class-question-pool-released-effective-july-1-2026" rel="noopener">details from ARRL</a>).
      Check the pool dates before buying anything for General or Extra, too.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cost" aria-labelledby="cost-h">
    <h2 id="cost-h">What does it cost, and what equipment do I need?</h2>
    <h3>Cost to get licensed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Studying:</strong> free with online practice tests, or the price of a book if you prefer paper.</li>
      <li><strong>Exam session fee:</strong> about $15 at many sessions.</li>
      <li><strong>FCC application fee:</strong> $35, paid after you pass.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Roughly <strong>$50</strong> to get licensed, plus any study materials. Both fees are subject to change.
    </p>
    <h3>Equipment</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Before the exam:</strong> nothing. You can study and even listen to ham radio without owning a radio.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Your first radio:</strong> most people start with a handheld radio (an <strong>HT</strong>, for
        handheld transceiver) for local repeaters and nets. Budget models cost roughly $30 and up, with
        better ones running a few hundred dollars.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Before you buy:</strong> ask around. Local hams are often glad to help you pick one, and some will
        let you try theirs.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Later:</strong> a better antenna, a mobile radio for the car, or a shortwave (HF) setup after you upgrade.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted">Prices are ballpark figures that change often. [TODO: confirm and adjust these ranges.]</p>
  </section>

  <section id="glossary" aria-labelledby="gloss-h">
    <h2 id="gloss-h">Glossary</h2>
    <dl class="glossary">
      <dt>Callsign</dt><dd>Your unique on-air identifier, issued by the FCC, like <span class="mono">{{ site.callsign }}</span>.</dd>
      <dt>FRN</dt><dd>FCC Registration Number. A free ID you get from the FCC before your first exam.</dd>
      <dt>HF</dt><dd>High Frequency, or shortwave (3–30 MHz). Signals can travel around the world.</dd>
      <dt>HT</dt><dd>Handheld transceiver. A walkie-talkie style ham radio.</dd>
      <dt>Net</dt><dd>A scheduled on-air meetup run by a host called net control.</dd>
      <dt>QSL</dt><dd>A confirmation of a two-way radio contact.</dd>
      <dt>QSO</dt><dd>A two-way radio conversation.</dd>
      <dt>Repeater</dt><dd>A relay station that rebroadcasts your signal to extend its range.</dd>
      <dt>ULS</dt><dd>The FCC's Universal Licensing System, the public license database.</dd>
      <dt>UHF / VHF</dt><dd>Ultra and Very High Frequency (300–3,000 MHz and 30–300 MHz). The bands handhelds use for local talk.</dd>
      <dt>VE / VEC</dt><dd>Volunteer Examiner, and the Volunteer Examiner Coordinator that accredits them.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

